---
import GuideLayout from "../../layouts/guide-layout.astro"
---
<GuideLayout
  title="Define your content"
  description="Content types are ordinary TypeScript. Describe fields, compile a snapshot once, and the CMS validates every entry against it."
  nextGuide={{ href: "/guides/providing-layers/", label: "Provide layers" }}
>
  <p>
    A blog post, author page, or product listing all start the same way: declare a content type in
    code, compile it, and pass the snapshot to your CMS layer.
  </p>

  <h2>Field helpers keep definitions readable</h2>
  <p>The library ships small helpers so you are not repeating field boilerplate:</p>
  <pre><code>{`import { ContentDefinition } from "nearly-headless-cms"

const { compileSnapshot, Fields } = ContentDefinition

const snapshot = compileSnapshot({
  definitionSpaceId: "example-blog",
  snapshotId: "example-blog-v1",
  definitions: [
    Fields.contentType({
      id: "post",
      name: "Post",
      history: true,
      fields: [
        Fields.requiredTextField("title", "Title", { maxLength: 180, minLength: 1 }),
        Fields.requiredSlugField("slug", "Slug"),
        Fields.requiredTextField("excerpt", "Excerpt", { multiline: true }),
        { key: "body", kind: { kind: "rich-text", formatVersion: 1 }, label: "Body", required: true },
        { key: "author", kind: Fields.relationship(["author"]), label: "Author", required: true },
        {
          key: "status",
          kind: Fields.enumField(["draft", "published"]),
          label: "Status",
          required: true,
          defaultValue: "draft",
        },
      ],
    }),
  ],
})`}</code></pre>

  <h2>One file for your model</h2>
  <p>
    In the Example CMS, every content type for this blog lives in
    <code>content/definitions.ts</code>. That file is the single source of truth for posts, authors,
    categories, tags, and comments. Keeping the model in one place makes it easy to explain and easy
    to change.
  </p>

  <h2>Compiled snapshots are deterministic</h2>
  <p>
    <code>compileSnapshot</code> validates relationships, field keys, and constraints, then produces
    an immutable snapshot with a stable fingerprint. Activate that snapshot in your persistence layer
    and every entry created afterward conforms to it.
  </p>
</GuideLayout>
